# This machine has core.autocrlf=true and the repo had no .gitattributes, which
# is a combination that has already cost this workspace a real incident: files
# correct in the repo, CRLF on disk, and reported as unmodified by every git
# command you would think to run. `.gitattributes` governs checkout, so it is
# the fix; hand-normalising is not, because a fresh clone brings it back.
#
# Scoped deliberately rather than applied as a blanket rule.

# Source and config: LF in the repo and on disk, everywhere.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary

# githooks/ holds a POSIX shell script plus plain-text list files that
# githooks/pre-push reads line by line. This machine has core.autocrlf=true, so
# without this rule they check out CRLF and `rebuild/v1\r` stops equalling
# `rebuild/v1` - the branch then falls through the owner-only list and is pushed
# as if it were a feature branch. Measured on Taskr 2026-08-05: the file was
# present, correct in the repo, `git status` clean, and the guard silently off.
#
# The hook strips a trailing CR itself as well. Neither half replaces the other:
# .gitattributes governs checkout and so fixes the files in repos that carry it,
# while the in-hook strip covers any clone or repo that does not.
/githooks/*    text eol=lf
